The key insight is that the `compute_max_radii` function has a greedy algorithm that may not be optimal. Let me look at it again:

```python
for i in range(n):
    for j in range(i + 1, n):
        dist = np.sqrt(np.sum((centers[i] - centers[j]) ** 2))

        # If current radii would cause overlap, reduce the larger one
        if radii[i] + radii[j] > dist:
            # Reduce the larger radius proportionally
            if radii[i] > radii[j]:
                radii[i] = dist - radii[j]
            else:
                radii[j] = dist - radii[i]
```

This greedy approach reduces the larger radius when circles overlap. This can be suboptimal because it might reduce a radius that was already constrained by another circle.

